<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

    <!--
      The cluster-backed half of the shared provider conformance suite, run against the
      monitor-workspace provider.

      ⚠ IT CONTAINED NO ASSERTIONS AND NO INFRASTRUCTURE UNTIL #32, AND STILL NO INFRASTRUCTURE.
      Everything the shared criteria run comes from test/CyberCloud.Cluster.Conformance; what lives
      here is class declarations over the SAME ProviderConformanceCase the Docker-free suite already
      uses — plus, since #32, the two tests in this tree that wait for a POD: the collector's, which
      POSTs an export to it, and Grafana's, which asks each provisioned datasource's health after the
      adversarial review found a Ready pod whose default datasource was dead. Both go through the API
      server's service proxy with ClusterConformanceHarness.CreateApiServerClient; PodDiagnostics is
      the one helper they share.

      ⚠ ONE CRD STUB, DERIVED, AND TWO KINDS THAT NEED NONE. A bare k3s serves
      /api/v1/namespaces/{ns}/configmaps and /secrets without being told; it serves no REST path for
      operator.victoriametrics.com/v1beta1 VMUser, and until the harness installs a definition the
      failure names nothing — the client cannot discover the group, so it throws
      k8s.Autorest.HttpOperationException with NO status code. ClusterConformanceHarness installs a
      minimal definition per custom kind, derived from the case's own Objects, which already carry
      group, version, kind and plural. There is no RequiredCrds member to declare and adding one is
      not the design.

      ⚠ IT IS A SEPARATE PROJECT FROM CyberCloud.Providers.Monitor.Conformance, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE
      SPLIT. That project must keep running on a machine with no Docker — it is the per-PR gate for
      every provider change. Adding Testcontainers to it would make the twelve-step write path, the
      verb grammar, the four reconciler clauses and the cross-tenant 404 all unrunnable without a
      daemon.

      ⚠ IT IS NOT NAMED `.Conformance.Cluster`. Directory.Build.props § Project role detection and
      build/Build.Test.cs § SuiteOwning both classify by the SUFFIX of the project name; a name ending
      in `.Cluster` is claimed by neither, so the project would build as a library rather than as a
      test host. `.Cluster.Conformance` is claimed by both with no edit to either file.
    -->

    <ItemGroup>
        <PackageReference Include="xunit.v3"/>
        <PackageReference Include="Shouldly"/>
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup Label="Global usings">
        <Using Include="Xunit"/>
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <!--
          ⚠ The CASE comes from the provider's Docker-free conformance project rather than being
          declared again here. One provider, one ProviderConformanceCase: a second copy would be a
          second description of the same provider, and the two would disagree the first time either
          changed.
        -->
        <ProjectReference
            Include="../CyberCloud.Providers.Monitor.Conformance/CyberCloud.Providers.Monitor.Conformance.csproj"/>
        <ProjectReference Include="../../../../test/CyberCloud.Cluster.Conformance/CyberCloud.Cluster.Conformance.csproj"/>
    </ItemGroup>

</Project>
